  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 241 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A slow moving upper level trough with associated closed \nsurface low is currently centered across the Ohio Valley and will \ntrack to the east northeast today into tomorrow. A baroclinic zone \nis also set up across the Great Lakes and eastern Ontario bringing \nsome isolated shower chances today across the St. Lawrence Valley. \nAs this system tracks east, cloud cover will increase areawide into \ntonight with south to southwest channeled flow just above the \nsurface, which should help limit, or prevent altogether, the \ncoverage of any potential fog tonight. \n\nInto tomorrow, the system will approach the St. Lawrence Valley and \nthe North Country with shower chances increasing by midday Sunday. \nThermodynamic profiles denote some skinny SBCAPE between 500-\n1000J/kg which will allow for at least some isolated convection and \nthunderstorm activity. This activity will likely be confined to near \nthe St. Lawrence River and along US-11 in St. Lawrence County. \nModest to weak mid level flow between 20-30 knots with Pwats \napproaching 1.8-2\", and deep warm layer cloud depths, locally heavy \nrain within any convection will be possible. WPC has kept the St. \nLawrence Valley in a Slight (Risk level 2 out of 4) for a at least a \n15% chance of exceeding flash flood guidance. The axis of these \nshowers remains somewhat variable from run to run across the Hi Res \nCAMs. The NAM3 keeps much of the convective activity during the day \ntomorrow over the border in Ontario, whereas the HRRR rides the axis \nof instability over western St. Lawrence County. Where this \ndeformation boundary sets up will have a large impact on the total \nrainfall from this system. Right now have kept the convective \nactivity on the US side of the border with a more stable run to run \nconsistent HRRR solution. Should an area see repeated rounds of \nshowers, especially with antecedent rainfall, flash flooding cannot \nbe ruled out. However, the St. Lawrence Valley at the present time \nis not expected to see significant enough rainfall to produce \nflooding over the prevailing flatter terrain. \n\nTowards Sunday evening and Sunday night, the deformation boundary \nwill shift east into the Champlain Valley and Vermont by Monday \nmorning. There is good agreement that overnight Sunday should have \nthe better rainfall coverage and contain the bulk of the rainfall. A \nfew areas of brief 0.25-0.5 inch/hour rates will be possible across \nnorthern New York within the boundary as it shifts east towards \nVermont. Showers will slowly taper off by Monday afternoon across \neastern Vermont with fog development likely in the valleys of the \nregion with brief high pressure nudging in. Currently have increased \nrainfall totals across the St. Lawrence Valley, with new guidance. \nTotal rainfall for most of the region will be around 0.5 inches. \nLocally higher amounts between an inch to an inch an a half will be \npossible from the foothills of the Adirondacks into the St. Lawrence \nValley, especially under any convective activity. Tuesday will be \ndrier and calmer with temperatures in the upper 70s to low 80s and \nclearing skies.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A slow moving to stationary front will be draped across our \nregion for the second half of next week bringing repeated rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms.  While the showers will linger through \nmultiple days, the thermodynamic profiles don't look to support severe \npotential as we are seeing 250-500 J/kg of CAPE each afternoon with \nweak shear. These values will support air mass/popcorn thunderstorms \nwith a few rumbles of thunder and short lived localized heavy rain. \nConsistent with a stationary front, temperatures will be remarkably \nconsistent over the period. Daytime highs in the warmest valleys will \nbe in the mid-80s daily with overnight lows falling into the mid-60s. \nThe stationary front will finally move off at the end of next week as a \nlow pressure will move in from the southeastern US bring more \norganization to the thunderstorms and the possibility of stronger \nconvection.",
